{"id":"neet-2026r-q001","pn":1,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Kinetic Theory","st":"Degrees of freedom, Cp/Cv","q":"An ideal gas is made of polyatomic molecules. Each of the molecules has three translational, three rotational and f number of vibrational modes. If the ratio of heat capacities Cₚ/Cᵥ of the gas is 8/7, then the value of f is:","o":["3","2","1","4"],"c":3,"s":"Internal energy of one mole: U = (3/2 k_B T + 3/2 k_B T + f k_B T) N_A.\nSo U = (3 + f) k_B T N_A = (3 + f)RT.\nCᵥ = dU/dT = (3 + f)R.\nCₚ = Cᵥ + R = (4 + f)R.\nCₚ/Cᵥ = (4 + f)/(3 + f) = 8/7 (given).\nSo f = 4."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q035","pn":35,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Kinetic Theory","st":"Mean free path","q":"The mean free path of molecules in an ideal gas A is half that of another ideal gas B. The diameter of the spherical molecules of gas A is twice the diameter of the molecules of B. If number densities of the gases A and B are nA and nB, respectively, the correct option is:","o":["nA = 2nB","nA = (1)/(4)nB","nA = (1)/(2)nB","nA = nB"],"c":2,"s":"Given λA = (1)/(2)λB and dA = 2dB.\nMean free path: λ = 1/(√2 π d² n).\nλA/λB = (1)/(2) = (dB² nB)/(dA² nA).\nSo nA/nB = 2·dB²/dA².\nPut dA = 2dB: nA/nB = 2dB²/(4dB²) = (1)/(2)."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q045","pn":45,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Kinetic Theory","st":"RMS speed","q":"A flask contains argon and chlorine in the ratio of 2:1 by mass. The temperature of the mixture is 27°C. The ratio of root mean square speed of the molecules of the two gases (V_rms(Ar)/V_rms(Cl)) is:\n(Atomic mass of argon = 40.0 u and molecular mass of chlorine = 70.0 u)","o":["√7/2","7/2","7/4","2/√7"],"c":0,"s":"v_rms = √(3RT/M).\nAt the same temperature, v_rms ∝ 1/√M.\nV_rms(Ar)/V_rms(Cl) = √(M_Cl/M_Ar) = √(70/40) = √7/2."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q014","pn":14,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Kinetic Theory","st":"Ideal gas equation","q":"An oxygen cylinder of volume 30 litre has 18.20 moles of oxygen. After some oxygen is withdrawn from the cylinder, its gauge pressure drops to 11 atmospheric pressure at temperature 27°C. The mass of the oxygen withdrawn from the cylinder is nearly equal to:\n[Given, R = (100/12) J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹, and molecular mass of O₂ = 32, 1 atm pressure = 1.01 × 10⁵ N/m]","o":["0.125 kg","0.144 kg","0.116 kg","0.156 kg"],"c":2,"s":"Number of moles left (absolute pressure = 11 + 1 = 12 atm):\nn = PV/RT = (12 × 1.01 × 10⁵ N/m² × 30 × 10⁻³ m³) / ((100/12) × 300)\nn = (12 × 1.01 × 12)/10 = 14.54 moles\nMoles removed = 18.2 − 14.54 = 3.656 moles\nMass removed = 3.656 × 32 = 116.99 g = 0.116 kg"},
{"id":"neet-2025-q029","pn":29,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Kinetic Theory","st":"Ideal gas equation, mixture of gases","q":"A container has two chambers of volumes V₁ = 2 litres and V₂ = 3 litres separated by a partition made of a thermal insulator. The chambers contain n₁ = 5 and n₂ = 4 moles of ideal gas at pressures p₁ = 1 atm and p₂ = 2 atm, respectively. When the partition is removed, the mixture attains an equilibrium pressure of","o":["1.3 atm","1.6 atm","1.4 atm","1.8 atm"],"c":1,"s":"P₁V₁ + P₂V₂ = P(V₁ + V₂).\n1(2) + 2(3) = P(2 + 3).\n8/5 = P.\nP = 1.6 atm."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q036","pn":36,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Kinetic Theory","st":"Gas laws, Charles's law","q":"The following graph represents the T-V curves of an ideal gas (where T is the temperature and V the volume) at three pressures P1, P2 and P3 compared with those of Charles’s law represented as dotted lines.\nThen correct relation is:","o":["P2 > P1 > P3","P1 > P2 > P3","P3 > P2 > P1","P1 > P3 > P2"],"c":1,"s":"Take one fixed temperature and draw a straight line parallel to the volume axis.\nAt the same temperature, the curve with the larger volume belongs to the lower pressure.\nHere V3 > V2 > V1.\nSo P1 > P2 > P3.","fig":{"w":260,"h":215,"alt":"T versus V graph with three solid curves labelled P1, P2 and P3, each with a dotted straight Charles-law line close to it; P1 is the steepest and leftmost, P3 the lowest and rightmost.","d":[["l",59,180,59,22,"a"],["l",59,180,250,180,"a"],["t",44,194,"0","e"],["p","M80 133 Q105 80 128 30"],["p","M80 153 L142 34","d"],["p","M86 141 C120 138 160 120 171 48"],["p","M95 158 L185 53","d"],["p","M94 158 C130 150 190 130 212 69"],["p","M105 169 L226 82","d"],["t",134,19,"P₁"],["t",189,40,"P₂"],["t",229,64,"P₃"],["t",33,106,"T","m"],["l",40,92,40,58,"a"],["t",168,203,"V","m"],["l",186,201,222,201,"a"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2023-q020","pn":20,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Kinetic Theory","st":"RMS speed and temperature","q":"The temperature of a gas is –50°C. To what temperature the gas should be heated so that the rms speed is increased by 3 times?","o":["223 K","669°C","3295°C","3097 K"],"c":2,"s":"v_rms = √(3RT/M), so v_rms ∝ √T.\nT₁ = 273 − 50 = 223 K.\nIncreased by 3 times is taken as final speed = v + 3v = 4v.\nv/4v = √(223/T₂), so 1/16 = 223/T₂.\nT₂ = 3568 K = 3568 − 273 = 3295°C."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q043","pn":43,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Kinetic Theory","st":"Ideal gas equation, volume of gas at STP","q":"The volume occupied by the molecules contained in 4.5 kg water at STP, if the intermolecular forces vanish away is","o":["5.6 × 10³ m³","5.6 × 10⁻³ m³","5.6 m³","5.6 × 10⁶ m³"],"c":2,"s":"Treat the molecules as an ideal gas: PV = nRT.\nMoles n = mass/molar mass = (4.5 × 10³)/18.\nAt STP T = 273 K and P = 10⁵ N/m².\nV = nRT/P = (4.5 × 10³/18) × (8.3 × 273)/10⁵ ≈ 5.66 m³."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q047","pn":47,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Kinetic Theory","st":"Rms speed of gases","q":"Three vessels of equal capacity have gases at the same temperature and pressure. The first vessel contains helium (monoatomic), the second contains fluorine (diatomic) and the third contains sulfur hexafluoride (polyatomic). The correct statement, among the following is:","o":["The root mean square speed of sulfur hexafluoride is the largest","All vessels contain unequal number of respective molecules","The root mean square speed of molecules is same in all three cases","The root mean square speed of helium is the largest"],"c":3,"s":"v_rms = √(3RT/M) does not depend on the degrees of freedom.\nAt the same T, the gas with the smallest molar mass has the largest v_rms.\nHelium has the smallest M, so its v_rms is the largest.\n(Equal V, P and T mean equal numbers of molecules, so option 2 is wrong.)"},
{"id":"neet-2021-q009","pn":9,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Kinetic Theory","st":"Kinetic theory formulas","q":"Match Column - I and Column - II and choose the correct match from the given choices.\nColumn-I: (A) Root mean square speed of gas molecules (B) Pressure exerted by ideal gas (C) Average kinetic energy of a molecule (D) Total internal energy of 1 mole of a diatomic gas\nColumn-II: (P) (1)/(3)nmv² (Q) √(3RT/M) (R) (5)/(2)RT (S) (3)/(2)kBT","o":["(A) - (R), (B) - (Q), (C) - (P), (D) - (S)","(A) - (R), (B) - (P), (C) - (S), (D) - (Q)","(A) - (Q), (B) - (R), (C) - (S), (D) - (P)","(A) - (Q), (B) - (P), (C) - (S), (D) - (R)"],"c":3,"s":"v_rms = √(3RT/M). A is Q.\nPressure of an ideal gas is P = (1)/(3)nmv² (n is molecules per volume). B is P.\nAverage kinetic energy per molecule is (3)/(2)kBT. C is S.\nU = (f/2)RT per mole; a diatomic gas has f = 5, so U = (5)/(2)RT. D is R.\nSo A-Q, B-P, C-S, D-R."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q096","pn":96,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Kinetic Theory","st":"Degrees of freedom","q":"The average thermal energy for a mono-atomic gas is : (k_B is Boltzmann constant and T absolute temperature)","o":["(3)/(2) k_BT","(5)/(2) k_BT","(7)/(2) k_BT","(1)/(2) k_BT"],"c":0,"s":"A monoatomic gas has 3 translational degrees of freedom.\nEach gives ½k_BT.\nAverage energy = (3/2)k_BT."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q098","pn":98,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Kinetic Theory","st":"Mean free path","q":"The mean free path for a gas, with molecular diameter d and number density n can be expressed as :","o":["1/(√2 nπd²)","1/(√2 n²πd²)","1/(√2 n²π²d²)","1/(√2 nπd)"],"c":0,"s":"Mean free path λ = 1/(√2 nπd²)."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q102","pn":102,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Kinetic Theory","st":"Ideal gas equation","q":"A cylinder contains hydrogen gas at pressure of 249 kPa and temperature 27°C. Its density is : (R = 8.3 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹)","o":["0.2 kg/m³","0.1 kg/m³","0.02 kg/m³","0.5 kg/m³"],"c":0,"s":"PV = (m/M)RT, so density ρ = PM/(RT).\nP = 249 × 10³ Pa, M = 2 × 10⁻³ kg/mol, T = 300 K.\nρ = (249 × 10³ × 2 × 10⁻³)/(8.3 × 300) = 498/2490 = 0.2 kg/m³."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q125","pn":125,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Kinetic Theory","st":"Temperature and kinetic energy","q":"Increase in temperature of a gas filled in a container would lead to -","o":["decrease in its pressure","decrease in intermolecular distance","increase in its mass","increase in its kinetic energy"],"c":3,"s":"Kinetic energy of gas molecules is proportional to the temperature.\nSo raising the temperature raises the kinetic energy.\nIn a fixed container the pressure also rises, and mass does not change."}
